<!--
    GH-8500: "stack address taken, stored, then dereferenced is mis-analysed."
    https://github.com/NationalSecurityAgency/ghidra/issues/8500

    Source (gcc -O0):
      int identity(int x) {
        int *p = &x;     // take the address of the (stack-resident) parameter
        *p = x;          // store THROUGH the pointer back into the stack slot
        return *p;        // read THROUGH the pointer
      }

    The address of the parameter's stack slot (s-0x1c) is spilled to another
    stack slot (s-0x8 = `p`).  The value is then stored through `p` (`*p = x`)
    and re-read through `p` (`return *p`).  Resolving the alias requires the
    indirect STORE/LOAD through `p` to be recognized as touching s-0x1c.

    Bug surface: the store-through-pointer was being dropped.  The STORE `*p = x`
    converts to a stack varnode (s-0x1c = x); if it is dead-code-eliminated one
    main-loop iteration BEFORE the aliasing LOAD `return *p` collapses to read
    s-0x1c, the load re-heritages s-0x1c with no reaching definition and the
    rendered C returns an undefined local (`return xStack_1c;`).

    Root cause (kuna): this race is exactly what Ghidra's heritage deadcode-delay
    restart is designed to defuse.  When `Heritage::removeRevisitedMarkers` runs
    AFTER a dead-removal, it calls `Heritage::bumpDeadcodeDelay`, which installs a
    one-pass deadcode delay on the affected space and requests a restart; the
    restart re-flows with the bumped delay so the LOAD resolves before the stack
    store can die.  In kuna that mechanism was BROKEN: `bumpDeadcodeDelay` was
    routed through a *fresh* `Override` each call, so its "delay already installed"
    suppression guard never fired and the bump re-requested a restart on every
    heritage pass.  The function therefore restart-looped until the driver's
    `MAX_REFLOW` budget exhausted, leaving the IR cleared (no structured blocks at
    all - `print C` rendered an empty `/* structuring declined at a seam */`
    shell, returning NEITHER the parameter nor the local).

    Fix: route `bumpDeadcodeDelay` through the function's PERSISTENT `Override`
    (which survives `Funcdata::clear()` across restart re-flows, exactly as C++
    `fd.getOverride()` does) and re-apply the persisted per-space delays to the
    `HeritageInfo` after `buildInfoList` on each pass (C++
    `Funcdata::startProcessing` then `Override::applyDeadCodeDelay`).  The bump now
    fires at most once, the restart converges, and the aliasing LOAD resolves
    BEFORE the stack store dies, so the store-through-pointer survives and the
    function correctly returns the parameter.

    Stage model: S3 dataflow / heritage deadcode-delay ordering.  This is a
    general heritage-ordering correctness fix; it is NOT gated on the
    `stack_alias_deadstore` arch flag.  The standard Ghidra deadcode-delay restart
    subsumes the previously-planned option-gated `holdStackAliasStores` hold for
    this minimized testcase, so `option stackalias on` is a no-op here and both
    passes recover the parameter.

    Pass 1 (default pipeline) now recovers the store-through-pointer: the function
    returns the parameter `a0`, and no uninitialized `xStack_*` local survives.
    Pass 2 (after `option stackalias on`) is identical (the option is subsumed).

    Disassembly (identity @ 0x1139):
      1139 55              PUSH RBP
      113a 4889e5          MOV  RBP,RSP
      113d 897dec          MOV  dword ptr [RBP + -0x14],EDI
      1140 488d45ec        LEA  RAX,[RBP + -0x14]
      1144 488945f8        MOV  qword ptr [RBP + -0x8],RAX
      1148 8b55ec          MOV  EDX,dword ptr [RBP + -0x14]
      114b 488b45f8        MOV  RAX,qword ptr [RBP + -0x8]
      114f 8910            MOV  dword ptr [RAX],EDX
      1151 488b45f8        MOV  RAX,qword ptr [RBP + -0x8]
      1155 8b00            MOV  EAX,dword ptr [RAX]
      1157 5d              POP  RBP
      1158 c3              RET
-->
